Alembic migrations of the backend (async dbapi; env.py builds the URL from DATABASE_*).

Policy: incremental migrations. A database is never recreated to take a schema change.

- versions/0001_initial.py (down_revision None) is the 2.0.0 schema: every table, index
  and constraint of that release, the row-level security policies of the agent role and
  its column grant on documents. It is FROZEN: never edit it.
- Every later schema change is a new hand-written revision with its own number
  (0002_..., 0003_...), whose down_revision is the previous head. Do not autogenerate
  it. Never edit a revision that has been applied anywhere.
- Keep the models (src/db/models.py) and sql-schema.json in step with the head revision.
- Write SQL as .sql files in migrations/sql/ that the revision reads with its own
  read_migration_sql (the revision never imports src/). A DROP statement may appear only in migrations/sql/ (sql-rules-drop-outside-migration).
  Give downgrade() a body that returns to the previous schema, and say in the revision
  docstring what data it loses.

On a schema change:

1. Edit the models and sql-schema.json, and add the revision.
2. Run `make db-upgrade` (repository root), or `specflow migrate`; the api container runs
   it on start. Deployed databases upgrade in place.
3. Check it: from backend/ `uv run --env-file ../.env.local alembic check` (the models
   match the revisions) and `uv run --env-file ../.env.local alembic current`, which
   prints the head, `0006_atlassian_connections (head)` at the time of writing.

Recreate a database only when it is stamped with a revision that no longer exists (for
example `0005` from the old chain): `alembic upgrade` fails there with "Can't locate
revision". Then `make down-volumes` and `make up`.

0002_project_collaborators is re-runnable (every statement is IF [NOT] EXISTS, or drops
and re-creates what it owns). Some databases were built from a temporary 0001_initial
that already held its objects and are stamped 0001_initial; for them 0002 changes
nothing. A new revision does not need this unless the same situation arises again.
